docs/Spec fixture changes: the post-sitting fixture, and the two drifts.

SUPERSEDES docs/plans/calendar_fixture_post_sitting_interval.patch, which carried only
the first of these three changes and is deleted in the same commit. Applying both would
conflict; apply this one.

docs/Spec/ is the locked canonical corpus and is read-only to Claude Code
(.claude/hooks/block-spec-and-secrets.mjs hard-blocks the write), so this is handed over
as a patch rather than committed.

  git apply docs/plans/calendar_spec_fixtures.patch

THREE CHANGES.

(1) A new fixture, `exam_post_sitting_interval_MonFri_FLSat_fortnightly`, appended after
    the last existing one. It is the first that can tell the post-exam anchor
    (`last_exam_date + interval_weeks x 7`) from the setup anchor: the real oracle places
    2026-10-03, an oracle with the branch deleted places 2026-09-26. Derived from
    `fallback_MonWedSat_60m_FLSat_after_exam` by changing four input fields.

(2) `fallback_MonWedSat_60m_FLSat_after_exam` gets `days_since_exam: 2` in place of
    `null`. `calendar_build_plan_input` emits that field and `last_completed_local_date`
    from ONE variable (20261011000000_exam_placement_frequency.sql:1093-1094), so a
    non-null date beside a null age is a shape production cannot produce. It changed no
    expectation only because that fixture is cold-start, where `weights()` returns before
    reading the field — measured, not assumed.

(3) The top-level `constants` block gains `default_full_length_weekday: 6`, which
    20261013000000 (#968) added to the oracle's `C` and this block did not. Inserted in
    the oracle's own key order, after its sibling.

VERIFIED against the live PL/pgSQL on PostgreSQL 17.11, for all 14 fixtures: every stored
`deterministic_v1`, `fallback_v1` and `exam_placement` reproduces from the reference, and
for the two fixtures this patch touches the RPC agrees with all three and
`calendar_validate_plan` accepts both generators. Every other byte of the file is
untouched — the splice was checked by re-parsing the result and asserting the other 13
fixtures compare equal.

NOTE ON (3). Nothing compares this block to the oracle's `C`, which is why it drifted
silently. An assertion in `calendar_parity_emit.py`, beside the `DOMAINS` one it already
makes, would make the drift impossible rather than merely corrected — offered, not done,
since it was outside what was asked.

Rationale and the census this came from: docs/plans/Calendar_Rule_Branch_Coverage_Census.md

